    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe rule \"reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with 400\" is now a new, self-contained validation step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline \u2014 mirroring exactly how the existing postcode rule is built, so it reads like the surrounding code.\n\nFive files, four new + one small wiring edit, no existing class grown:\n\n1. **`RequireCurrentRegistrationDate.java`** (new function) \u2014 reads the already-published `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, throws when `registrationDate` is after `LocalDate.now()`. Single method, CC 3, no surrounding class complexity.\n2. **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** (new checked exception).\n3. **`FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** (new handler) \u2014 responds `400`.\n4. **`...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml`** (new escalation mapping).\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted one `registrationDate` step between `validate` and `postcode` (a 4-line addition).\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nEvery function I touched is a brand-new, single-method class with `WMC_other \u2248 0`, so the dominant cost lever (the surrounding class complexity you must hold) stays at its floor. I deliberately did **not** add the check to `RequireOwnerFields` (WMC_other = 3 from `addIfBlank`, higher CC) or to `BuildOwner`, which would have paid for that surrounding context and grown an existing method. The new step slots in after the body is validated/published (so it reads via `@Val`) and before `build`, so a future date never reaches persistence \u2014 an invalid body returns 400, consistent with the postcode/missing-fields rules.",
